# 9. Conditions Required for Proof

The theory would gain strong support if one or more of the following were experimentally verified:

## 9.1 Proton Anisotropy

High-energy scattering experiments should reveal measurable non-spherical tetrahedral structure in the proton.

Observable consequences may include:

* orientation-dependent scattering,
* hexagonal projection signatures,
* or preferred angular resonances.

---

## 9.2 Gauge Resonance Structure

If the vacuum possesses a fixed geometric propagation scale, electromagnetic fields may exhibit resonance structures near harmonics or subharmonics of

[
\nu_{\mathrm{vac}}
==================

2.99792458\times10^{23},\mathrm{Hz}
]

---

## 9.3 Non-Spherical Radius Measurements

Different proton radius experiments should converge toward distinct geometric radii rather than a single spherical radius.

Specifically:

* muonic hydrogen measurements may probe inscribed geometry,
* while scattering experiments may probe projected or circumscribed geometry.

---

## 9.4 Quantized Gauge Area

If electromagnetic quanta possess invariant area rather than variable size, deviations from standard renormalization assumptions may emerge at ultra-high energy density.

